462:
A person shall be guilty of disorderly conduct if, with the purpose of causing public danger, alarm, disorder, nuisance, or if his conduct is likely to cause public danger, alarm, disorder or nuisance, or if he willfully does any of the following acts in a public place:
(1) Commits an act in a violent and tumultuous manner toward another whereby that other is placed in danger of his life, limb or health;
(2) Commits an act in a violent and tumultuous manner toward another whereby the property of any person is placed in danger of being destroyed or damaged;
(3) Causes, provokes or engages in any fight, brawl, or riotous conduct so as to endanger the life, limb, health or property of another;
(4) Interferes with another's pursuit of lawful occupation by acts of violence;
(5) Obstructs, either singly or together with other persons, the flow of vehicular or pedestrian traffic and refuses to clear such public way when ordered to do so by the sheriff or his deputies or other lawful authority known to be such;
(6) Is in a public place under the influence of an intoxicating liquor or drug in such condition as to be unable to exercise care for his own safety or the safety of others;
(7) Resists or obstructs the performance of duties by sheriff or his deputies or any other authorized official of the county, when known to be such an official;
(8) Incites, attempts to incite, or is involved in attempting to incite a riot;
(9) Addresses abusive language or threats to any member of the sheriff or his deputies, any other authorized official of the county who is engaged in the lawful performances of his duties, or any other person when such words have a tendency to cause acts of violence. Words merely causing displeasure, annoyance or resentment are not prohibited;
(10) Damages, befouls or disturbs public property or the property of another so as to create hazardous, unhealthy or physically offensive conditions;
(11) Makes or causes to be made any loud, boisterous and unreasonable noise or disturbance to the annoyance of any other persons nearby, or near to any public highway, road, street, lane, alley, park, square or common, whereby the public peace is broken or disturbed, or the traveling public is annoyed;
(12) Fails to obey a lawful order to disperse by a police officer, when known to be such an official, where one or more persons are committing acts of disorderly conduct in the immediate vicinity, and the public health and safety is imminently threatened;
(13) Uses of abusive or obscene language or makes an obscene gesture.
(Ord. of 2-22-2005(2), §§ 1, 2)

475:
The director shall plan, promote, organize and supervise a comprehensive county recreation program and administer the same in the interest of all of the citizens of the county, and shall have, in addition to other responsibilities as may be assigned to it from time to time, primary responsibility for the following:
(1) Design of all parks, parkways and public grounds;
(2) The care, maintenance and landscaping of all public lands, including parks, parkways, recreation complexes, and playgrounds;
(3) Organization and programming of all recreational activities involving general public participation in a comprehensive and diversified program, including athletics, senior citizen programs, arts and crafts, and other such programs; and,
(4) Preparation and presentation of an annual departmental budget to be considered by the board of commissioners in connection with the development of the county's annual budget and the management of department's finances within the budget so established.
(Ord. No. 2003-02, § 3, 2-25-2003)

484:
All persons, prior to engaging in the harvesting of any timber, either logs or pulpwood, in the county, are required to notify the public works director, or his designee, in person or by telephone, and provide the following information:
(1) The name of the person about to engage in the harvesting operation;
(2) The mailing address and telephone number of the harvesting entity;
(3) The name of the foreman or supervisor personally in charge of the harvesting operation;
(4) The name of the landowner where the harvesting operation is to take place;
(5) The access points (within a reasonable area of operation) to a public county road from the lands on which the harvesting is to take place; and
(6) The date on which the harvesting operation will commence and the anticipated duration of the operation.
The public works director, or his designee, shall inspect the access site to the public county road and shall determine its suitability and if a culvert is required at the point of access to the county right-of-way, together with the size and length thereof, if needed. The culvert must be installed by the harvesting person under the supervision of the public works director or his designee.
(Ord. No. 92-02, § I, 11-12-1991)

557:
The public health, safety, economy, good order, appearance, convenience, morals, and general welfare require the harmonious, orderly and progressive development of land within the county. In furtherance of this general intent, the regulation of land development by the county is adopted for the following purposes:
(1) To encourage land development in an orderly, serviceable, and equitable fashion;
(2) To ensure the timely provision of required streets utilities, other facilities and services to new land developments;
(3) To ensure the adequate provision of safe and convenient traffic access and circulation, both vehicular and pedestrian, in and through new land developments;
(4) To ensure the provision of needed public open spaces and building sites in new land developments through the dedication or reservation of land for recreational, educational, and other public purposes; and
(5) To ensure, in general, the wise and timely development of new, undeveloped areas in harmony with the comprehensive plan of the county.
(Ord. No. 97-08, § 1.4, 6-10-1997)

