(a) Scale. The sketch plan shall be drawn at a scale of not less than 100 feet to one inch.
(b) Vicinity map. The sketch plan shall include a vicinity map at a scale not less than one inch equals one mile showing the relationship of the proposed subdivision or development to surrounding development.
(c) Other requirements. The sketch plan shall show the following:
(1) Proposed name of subdivision or development;
(2) Name, address and telephone number of petitioner and architect, surveyor, engineer or designer;
(3) All names and addresses of all property owners of the site;
(4) All names and addresses, as listed by the county assessor, of all owners of the land adjacent to the site and location of those property lines;
(5) North indicator;
(6) Date of preparation:
(7) Total acreage of tract to be subdivided or developed, lot layout, and estimated lot sizes or development site.
(8) Zoning district classification of land to be subdivided or developed and adjoining land;
(9) Existing and proposed uses of land throughout the subdivision or development including streets, roads, and land reserved or dedicated for public or common use;
(10) In case of a resubdivision, a copy of the existing plat with proposed resubdivision superimposed thereon;
(11) Location of existing buildings on the property to be subdivided or developed.
(12) Location of city limit lines and county lines, if applicable;
(13) Sketch plan shall be on one sheet where practical. If the complete plat cannot be shown on one sheet, it may be shown on more than one sheet with an index map on a separate sheet of the same size.
(d) Subdivision of part of property. The subdivider or developer shall submit a sketch plan of such subdivider's or developer original tract even though such subdivider's or developer present plans call for the actual development of only a part of the property.
(Ord. No. 97-08, § 5.1, 6-10-1997; Ord. of 7-12-05, § 4; Ord. of 2-28-2006, § 3; Ord. of 4-13-2021(1), § 2)

594:
(a) In certain circumstances, the board of commissioners may approve the construction of a private street within a subdivision or development. A private street is a street that is not accepted by the county as part of the county road system and is privately owned and maintained. Such streets or roads shall be constructed to the same standards and shall meet all other requirements with regard to road construction as is provided in this chapter for public streets or roads, including, but not limited to, the requirement for a minimum right-of-way of 60 feet. In addition, the developer shall:
(1) Provide the county with a written release of maintenance agreement, acceptable to the county Board of Commissioners, releasing the county from responsibility for present or future maintenance and upkeep of the roadway;
(2) Erect a sign indicating the existence of a private street at the intersection of each public street with a private street;
(3) Include a disclosure statement on all sales agreements, deeds and plats relating to the subdivision or its lots indicating that the street or road is a private street, that the county has no responsibility for its maintenance or upkeep, the nature of the ownership of the street and responsibility for its maintenance and upkeep, and the rights of purchasers of lots in the subdivision to enforce such provisions.
(b) Such streets shall be platted and deeded to a responsible association or entity, such as a homeowners association, approved by the board of commissioners. The board of commissioners may require, as a condition to approval of a private street or road:
(1) That the developer provide a specific maintenance plan or financial guarantee of proper maintenance and repair;
(2) Access easements in favor of the county and its various departments and agencies; or
(3) Such other concessions as are deemed appropriate for the public good and the benefit of the lot owners.
(Ord. No. 97-08, § 6.6, 6-10-1997; Ord. of 4-13-2021(1), § 2)

650:
There is hereby authorized to be levied and collected within the county, as provided in O.C.G.A. § 48-8-110 et seq., a special one percent sales and use tax for the following purposes and in the following amounts:
(1) Construction of roads, streets and bridges: $2,484,000.00;
(2) Construction of improvements to the county jail and capital expenditures related to public safety: $2,173,500.00;
(3) Capital improvements to the recreational complex: $993,600.00;
(4) Capital improvements to the county hospital: $496,800.00; and
(5) Capital improvements to the county library: $62,100.00.
The tax shall be imposed for a period of five years for road, street and bridge construction purposes and for five years for the other stated purposes, or until the maximum cost of those projects as stated has been realized from the net proceeds of the tax.
(Res. No. 2001-04, 4-10-2001)
